<TopicPage slug="subjects/human-resources/foundational/hr-s-role-and-legal-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Human resources management is the organisational function for the people system: acquiring,
    developing, rewarding and fairly treating the workforce. Its foundation is legal as much as
    managerial — employment decisions are the most litigated acts in business, and the floor
    under every HR practice is the anti-discrimination and labour law of its jurisdiction.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the legal architecture under every people decision" widget="equationStep" config={lawCfg} />

  <h2>Disparate impact: discrimination without intent</h2>
  <p>
    The US doctrine born from Griggs v. Duke Power (1971): a neutral requirement — a diploma, a
    physical test, an algorithm — becomes unlawful if it excludes a protected group and is not
    demonstrably job-related. This is why modern HR validates selection criteria with data. The
    principle has spread globally as the statistical study of hiring outcomes became standard.
  </p>

  <h2>HR's domains, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    The SHRM BASK organises the craft into people (acquisition, development, rewards), organisation
    (structure, culture, effectiveness) and workplace (law, risk, engagement) — with analytics
    running through all three. Each later unit of this programme takes one domain; this one
    establishes the frame: HR decisions are business decisions carrying legal weight, executed
    with a duty of fairness.
  </p>

  <h2>Why HR is strategic, not clerical</h2>
  <p>
    Labour is most firms' largest cost and their least replicable advantage; hiring and culture
    compound like interest. The modern HR case is evidential: selection validity, engagement
    correlations, turnover costs. The clerical-HR caricature survives in bad organisations. In
    good ones, HR knows which decisions moved the numbers — and can prove it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SHRM BASK; Griggs v. Duke Power Co. (1971); Dessler, <em>Human Resource Management</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
